+### [Generalized Trilinear Interpolation](9462-generalized-trilinear-interpolation.md)
+Generalized Trilinear Interpolation (GTI) is a reconstruction method for cell-centered Block-Structured Adaptive Mesh Refinement (BS-AMR) data that computes field values at arbitrary points, specifically the vertices of dual cells or octants, ensuring $C^0$ continuity across refinement level boundaries. It solves the problem where standard trilinear interpolation fails because neighbors at different resolution levels are missing or misaligned, by using virtual unstructured elements to initialize octant vertices with precomputed weights derived from a multivariate polynomial basis. It builds on [Octant Method](2648-octant-method.md) by providing the vertex initialization step required before trilinear interpolation within an octant can proceed.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method yields better rendering quality and performance compared to sample-based isosurface methods in OSPRay, which suffer from poor quality and performance on AMR data [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Continuous interpolation across level boundaries; rectilinear octants simplify ray-triangle intersection tests; no need for unstructured mesh stitching; adaptive to refinement levels [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Sacrifices accuracy at boundaries to maintain continuity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When continuous, high-quality isosurface rendering of BS-AMR data is required, particularly for ray tracing [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When maximum accuracy at level boundaries is prioritized over continuity [S1, unofficial].
